    <div class="section-title">3. Affordability and Rent Burden Analysis</div>
    <p class="section-desc">The single most actionable metric for policymakers: rent as a share of monthly income.</p>

    <p class="section-intro">
      Housing affordability analysis reveals a counterintuitive but critical finding: <strong>high-income cities often exhibit worse rent-to-salary ratios than lower-income ones</strong>. Seattle (71.2%), Zurich (70.8%), Geneva (70.2%), and Lausanne (70.1%) top the rent burden ranking — not because residents are poor, but because housing market constraints have allowed rents to outpace even generous salaries. Meanwhile, the global average of 61.5% signals that a majority of the 80 cities surveyed require residents to spend more than half their income on housing. No city falls below 51%.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      ⚠️ <strong>Policy threshold:</strong> Rent-to-salary ratios above 50% are generally considered severe by housing economists. <strong>All 80 cities in this dataset exceed this threshold</strong>, indicating a systemic global affordability crisis — not a localized anomaly.
