    <ul class="findings-list">
      <li><strong>Finding 1 — Universal Rent Burden Crisis:</strong> All 80 cities in the dataset record rent-to-salary ratios above 50%, indicating that housing affordability has crossed a systemic threshold globally. The mean of 61.5% suggests the average urban resident worldwide must allocate more than three-fifths of their income to rent alone, leaving inadequate margin for food, healthcare, and savings.</li>
      <li><strong>Finding 2 — Affluent Cities Paradox:</strong> The 4 cities with the highest rent-to-salary ratios (Seattle 71.2%, Zurich 70.8%, Geneva 70.2%, Lausanne 70.1%) are also among the wealthiest in the world. High absolute wages have not insulated residents from housing market pressures — this signals that supply-side housing policy failures are the primary driver, not income levels.</li>
      <li><strong>Finding 3 — Tier 5 Hyperinflation Crisis (14 Cities):</strong> The 14 cities classified as Tier 5 share a convergent profile: avg inflation 28.4%, avg PPP 48.0, and avg salary $430/month. This group spans four continents — South Asia (6 cities), Latin America (2), Africa (3), and the Middle East/Europe (3) — indicating this is a global, not regional, phenomenon requiring coordinated multilateral response.</li>
      <li><strong>Finding 4 — Oil Exposure as Inflation Amplifier:</strong> High oil-shock exposure cities average 20.4% annual inflation versus 3.4% for low-exposure cities — a 6x differential. Energy policy (subsidies, renewables transition, diversification) is therefore among the highest-leverage interventions available to governments in exposed regions.</li>
      <li><strong>Finding 5 — Southeast Asia Bifurcation:</strong> Singapore (CoL 87.7, PPP 105.5) sits 40+ points above the regional average, representing an outlier that inflates regional metrics. Bangkok, Manila, Jakarta, and Kuala Lumpur all cluster in the 37–48 CoL range with PPP between 52–88. Policy analysis for this region should treat Singapore separately from the rest of the Southeast Asian urban cluster.</li>
      <li><strong>Finding 6 — Middle East Divergence:</strong> The Middle East has the widest within-region PPP spread of any region: Tel Aviv (PPP 109.5) versus Beirut (PPP 35.0) — a 74.5-point gap within a single region. This reflects the combined effect of conflict, monetary policy collapse, and petro-wealth concentration that defines the geopolitical fault lines of the region.</li>
      <li><strong>Finding 7 — Eastern Europe Under Pressure:</strong> Kyiv's classification as Tier 5 (War-Driven Crisis, 14.5% inflation) is a distinct outlier from its Eastern European peers (Prague, Warsaw, Budapest all Tier 3). Moscow and St. Petersburg (Tier 4, 9.5% inflation) show the sanctions impact on purchasing power. This region requires distinct country-level analysis rather than aggregate treatment.</li>
      <li><strong>Finding 8 — North America Rent Crisis:</strong> With an average rent-to-salary ratio of 67.0% — the highest of any major region — North American cities face a structural housing crisis. Vancouver (65.8%, labeled "Severe Housing") and New York (62.9%) have the additional burden of inflated rent indices (63–100). Policy interventions targeting housing supply, rent stabilization, and inclusionary zoning are most urgently needed here.</li>
    </ul>

